  "verbalization": "The visible evidence suggests that cocaine may interact with Extracellular signal-regulated kinase (ERK) through multiple indirect pathways. The drug is known to target E-selectin, which in turn is a target of Isodalbergin and Meridine, both of which have ERK as their target. This indicates that cocaine can influence ERK through different downstream molecules, potentially via distinct biological mechanisms. Additionally, cocaine is also linked to PITSLRE serine/threonine-protein kinase CDC2L2, which is targeted by Parritadial, further expanding the possible pathways through which cocaine could modulate ERK activity. These findings collectively suggest that cocaine may exert its effects on ERK through a network of intermediate targets, highlighting the complexity of the drug-target relationship. The evidence supports the possibility that cocaine influences ERK through multiple routes, but the exact nature of these interactions remains to be fully elucidated.",
